## Lore

When Melbourne and Perth walled themselves in and left everyone else outside,
they earned the lasting hatred of the Outback. Around P.A. 70 a few people on
the coast near Melbourne decided to follow the example of Wade, the folk
hero said to have been first to breach the walls and drive out with a truck
full of weapons, E-Clips, armor and electronics. The Raider, also called the
Tech-Raider, City-Raider or Good Raider, is the kind of bandit that grew from
that story: a thief daring enough to break into a Tech-City, one of its
military camps or one of its convoys.

Few are anything like Robin Hood. Most are highly skilled cat burglars with
commando habits who steal for their own profit and for the thrill of crawling
through a minefield under laser nets and patrols just to reach the outer
wall. On the job they are all business. To most Outbackers anyone who can rob
the Cities is a hero all the same, and whatever a Raider cannot use sells
quickly to a market hungry for anything high-tech.

## GM Notes

Only Advanced Math gets a bonus among the Science skills: +15% when taken as
an O.C.C. Related Skill. The sheet applies no Science bonus, so add it by
hand.

The Raider takes no Secondary Skills at level one; two each at levels 2, 5,
10 and 15.

Selling stolen goods (printed 72-73): sold direct, an item fetches list price
up to 200% more, depending on the client and the item, but most Outbackers
are poor, so it may go for 10-40% under list, and selling direct is dangerous
near the Cities. A fence pays 20-30% of list, or up to 50% for something rare
or in demand. City Traders report suspected Raiders to their City.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: the night suit (a black jump suit
with gloves, quiet rubber-soled boots, a balaclava and a climbing harness;
the light armor''s mask carries passive nightvision goggles and a radio
scanner), a set of city clothes that would fall apart within days in the
Outback, various harnesses and belts, a wristwatch, a glass cutter and an
M.D. energy pistol. The 1D4 grenades may be hand grenades, smoke grenades or
both.

Back at his lair he is likely to have half a dozen M.D. weapons, a dozen
S.D.C. weapons, a crossbow, wooden stakes, a light to medium suit of
environmental M.D.C. armor (perhaps of the kind City police or military
wear), an M.D.C. flak vest, 1D4 portable hand-held computers, 1D4 CD players
and recorders, a language translator, infrared distancing binoculars, a
flashlight, a dozen note pads, a box of 24 pens and markers, an air filter,
suitcase, satchel, briefcase (10 M.D.C.), backpack, sleeping bag, tent, basic
and personal items, and perhaps various disguises.

Vehicle: starts with a jet pack and a hovercycle or motorcycle.

Money: besides the cash, 1D4x$1000 in tradeable goods; he has spent the rest
on equipment and the high life. NPC villains, typically anarchist or evil,
have 3-5 times more.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'raider-australia');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'raider-australia';

